ults  but even before we had uh the  exporting we supported already for a  while the fbx  and the collider so they could even take  it to blender  where they already also support the  right tracing so there's  there's lots of options for our users to  to benefit from that technology the  whole reason  we use visualization tools is to  actually  better design and provide better  services to our clients which you  touched on briefly  i guess it'd be interesting to find out  what your opinion is  on and how we can enhance the client's  experience  so for example because twin motion and  unreal are  very quick and very simple to do you can  very easily start  rendering very early on you don't have  to finish the  design completely and then give them the  final product moments before it's about  to go to construction and then they want  to change everything  so in your opinion do you think the  future of ray tracing is helping  us design and create 3d visualizations  earlier on  so clients can have a better experience  at the end of the day  ray tracing i wouldn't look it up i mean  i wouldn't think of  of a way to to help your  your race association it's just a  technology that is to  work more efficient uh like i said  you know before if you run uh back then  i would say maybe three  years ago if you wanted to render  something you had to wait  uh quite a long long long time and also  uh the denoising the all the all this  stuff is  to work more efficient and  have a result a lot faster and also  minimizing the budget that you put in  because  often lots of architects didn't want to  really invest  on this photorealistic because it just  it was too time-consuming  and it was too it cost too much and they  figure out that the clients as long as  they have some  some materials and something that  simulate what they  the end result would be they were happy  enough but now we can  give them something better now we can  in in such a fast uh like with twin  motion all you have to really is just  press a button  and you have already a virtual  environment so  okay in that sense then it facilitates  a little bit more um to analyze a few  things such as the shading where the sun  is  are you happy with with how the the the  song comes in in certain areas  but the ray tracing i see a more of an  efficiency  now i guess this is a bit of a off-topic  question and sorry about this  but do you guys at brix cad actually  have the opportunity to talk to people  at unreal engine and twin motion and  luminance  to have your opinion presented to them  and have your  voices heard to be able to actually  create what you want to create in their  software  yeah i didn't follow closely the lumia  but i did follow uh closely the two  motion  and yes we had a direct uh connection  they were very  very helpful uh and every now and then  whenever we spotted a bug  or things were a bit different or  we wanted to behave a bit differently  they  um yeah there were easy access  they were willing to work with us  cannot complain i was actually very  pleased and the surprise of  how fast the collaboration was because i  think within the  three or four months everything was uh  ready to go  okay um i guess we'll step away from  visualization now and move towards  parametric design parametric design is  something  i've been fascinated about since i was  at uni and it's something that can  create  incredible things it is time consuming  but  rewarding at the same time parametric  design is is something we truly use in  big firms it's not something we use  in little firms and i've worked in both  i've  now worked in a smaller firm it's  probably more of a medium firm  and we're trying to push into the  parametric design but it's  hard to convince people that it's of  value  similar to how visualization was  previously it was  wasn't of value because it took too long  and clients were happy with  a little material here and there how do  you see parametric design in the future  stepping away from just these large  firms  to be able to actually be integrated  into a smaller firm and to help speed up  the documentation process i guess yeah  that's  an interesting question and i as  i'm a very parametric  like parametric design enthusiast and  generative design enthusiast  um i i will take this question um  so also same like you i  have been interested in this since my  university  studies i have been introduced there to  parametric design and i guess  that's for a lot of people the first  touch they  have with parametric design but in  brixis i could elaborate  that passion a little bit further so  that  i was actually still working as a  student here in brixis  and i was yeah i was  given the tasks of designing something  designing the conference building  actually or the conference model train  station  if you look it up it's from a few years  back or two years back  and i was designed to to design this and  actually  what i've found out since then is that  parametric design has  a lot of um applications so not only  can you do these fancy rail station  canopies and and all of those things but  you can also  use it for some smaller scale  models or some smaller scale use cases i  particularly find it useful to use  parametric design  whenever you um want to  yeah want to make a unique situation you  want to make  you it needs to adapt to a unique  situation your design and i guess that's  in a lot of  small firm cases exactly the the fact  that you  will have your clients with all their  unique needs and all their unique  situations  and actually whenever you would use a  parametric design  in smaller scale projects you could  in my opinion have  this parametric design adapt to these  unique situations  yet um you reuse certain parts namely  the scripting that's behind it you  you just reuse it but by tweaking the  input parameters you can get  unique situations unique solutions  actually to  to the problem at hand so um i guess  that  um whenever you mix um  parametric and generative design  together with  a a good validation step of the  architects  you have these unique situations that  are actually fairly easy to to make  at first you might seem that you need to  invest a little bit of more time to  to design something by scripting it  but in the end you will be able to reuse  it you will be able to  um yeah adjust it slightly and one of  the  most use cases would be for example  stair designs  and you can you can adapt the stair  you can make it fully fit the situation  at  hand yet it doesn't have to cost as much  when you manufacture it because also in  the generative design process  you can immediately put in that step  where you  validate whether you can make it with  these standard diced parts so you just  use some standardized part to create a  very unique situation that's  all possible with a generative design as  well  and i think that is mainly where  smaller firms can benefit also another  example would be  wood framing in houses whenever your  window just changes slightly your wood  framing structure needs to  needs to adapt and that's also possible  by scripting  your you your wood framing that it just  adapts or your wall cladding if you want  a nice cladding design for one of your  interior walls  um like there are a lot of use cases and  and  i think on the bricks's website there  will be soon  and a special page for this so you will  be able to check out  some of the ideas but as well we are  collaborating with  mcneil here um to to integrate  grasshopper in bricscad and  um that's um also a wealth of  information  is on the mcneil community's sites  so um you you will find these  mind-boggling scripts  and that i always like to watch that uh  just as well and i guess that's a good  step for people to get interested in  it's just watching the nice results  i think staying on the smaller jobs  smaller firms topic  recently i've had a few jobs hit my desk  at least that  have been asked to be produced under bim  but  all the consultants on the team have  been a little bit older and a little bit  more set in their ways  so they've refused to  step up and use the bim software and  they just want to continue with their 2d  drawings and their 2d lines  some of them are still drawing by hand  so it's incredible to see these guys  still going and they  are a wealth of knowledge so you get so  much information out of them  but at the same time you're unable to  actually produce what the client wants  so how do you see those that older  generation i guess  of architects and engineers stepping  away from 2d  and embracing bim for me at least  maybe melissa can add to what i say here  but for me at least about showing them  the value of them  again and also the potential ease with  they can  they can start with it we would say  or at least i would say start simple  don't try to  start with your cla with a large client  saying okay can we do this entire site  or in in bim maybe that will be uh too  much of a step so i would start  say start simple um and um  just yeah let let you guide also  by by maybe the younger generation that  is um educated in it even maybe  the the older generation is just not  educated in it but you can learn a lot  from each other i think  um like bim has its flaws still as well  so  um for example when it comes to  detailing  pen and paper or maybe still um the  first tools that you should grab  so i think um whenever there is a nice  collaboration between these generations  they can actually  produce something which is very valuable  where you can have these  um talks about um how can we  transform what does that idea on paper  to  something which is um already starting  in bim  and i think in bricks in bricscad bim  we are like melissa already said we are  trying to pick up on that note um  with saying okay you can just start  modeling  you can start modeling a free form  almost um yeah you can push pull you can  extrude stuff  and then you can add the bim data later  like you don't have to worry about  getting everything right from the first  moment that you start your design  you can let it just flow according to  this  workflow that you um feel comfortable  with and i think that's  for me a big uh step to convincing um  everyone actually to to start with bim  is um  making them see the value first  educating them uh secondly and then  thirdly just  starting small and starting with some  simple projects  trying it out seeing what values it can  give them  and and so on so i don't know if melissa  had something to add  i think it's just a little bit  reinforcement of what like floyd you  said  to um i mean obviously is an old  generation so they know already about  bim for a long time  um but when it started it was revit uh  the one  mainly providing this a solution and  yeah revit is known for it not being so  easy  takes a lot of learning takes a lot of  budget especially  if if you have uh if you want to start  you  the complete firm to do it is really a  lot of cost  that they have to do uh to have  everybody on board  so maybe just to to have them see  that there's no longer uh revit uh  taking part on this bim  and the bim is not revit bim  is completely separate from revit revit  just provide you the solution for  for the model itself and  yeah maybe with uh or or solution  the fact that if it follows a little bit  of a logical  workflow you know if they can do 2d they  can then do 3d  and then they can just um  classify things however the one so  i mean i feel it's a bit uh  i mean maybe it might be seen as bias  but it makes a bit of sense  you know almost everybody starts with 2d  at school and then  you do some 3d and yeah the  bim actually if you look at our  perspective it's just classifying  uh the the the solids and then you can  extract the data however you want it  i think i think that's a good point  understanding that revit used to be  everything it used to be the only thing  people used in firms it was the best for  bim it was the best for everything  and it's just interesting how the world  progressed and how more and more  software has come out but  now you don't have to use revit you have  so many options  that everybody has an opinion of what  the best one is and  everybody always will and that's okay  but  i think that's probably one of the main  sticking points is when you do speak to  the older generation  they're just thinking revit in the back  of their head and it's so complex and  so difficult to use and so difficult to  learn so i think that's an excellent  point  but anyway that is all the questions i  had for you guys today  so you can relax and be stress-free you  don't have to be on camera anymore  um thank you so much for taking the time  i do appreciate it  okay okay cool thank you thank you for  the interview  anyway that's all for me today guys  thank you so much for watching i hope  you enjoyed the video  i hope you enjoyed the interview with  melissa and floor and i hope you got  some  great valuable information out of it if  you did make sure you smash that  subscribe button down below  because as always i'll see you next  monday
 

